The expansion in length due to thermal energy is called linear expansion.
If the substance is in the form of a long rod of length l, then for small change \Delta T, in temperature, the fractional change \Delta l/l, in length (shown in Fig.7.4), is directly proportional to \Delta T.
\frac{\Delta l}{l} \propto \Delta T
or \frac{\Delta l}{l} = \alpha \Delta T --- (7.10)
where \alpha is called the coefficient of linear expansion of solid. Its value depends upon nature of the material. Rearranging Eq. (7.10), we get
\alpha = \frac{\Delta l}{l\Delta T}
= \frac{l_{\rm T} - l_{\rm 0}}{l_{\rm 0}(T - T_{\rm 0})} \qquad --- (7.11)
where l_0 = length of rod at 0 °C l_T = length of rod when heated to T °C
T_0 = 0 °C is initial temperature T = final temperature
\Delta l = l_{\rm T} - l_{\rm 0} = change in length
\Delta T = T - T_0 = \text{rise in temperature}
Referring to Eq. (7.11), if l_0=1 and T-T_0=1 °C, then
\alpha = l_{\rm T} - l_0 (numerically).
Coefficient of linear expansion of a solid is thus defined as increase in the length per unit original length at 0 ^{\circ}C for one degree centigrade rise in temperature.
The unit of coefficient of linear expansion is per degree celcius or per kelvin. The magnitude of \alpha is very small and it varies only a little with temperature. For most practical purposes, \alpha can be assumed to be constant for a particular material. Therefore, it is not necessary that initial temperature be taken as 0 °C. Equation (7.11) can be rewritten as
\alpha = \frac{l_2 - l_1}{l_1(T_2 - T_1)} --- (7.12)
where l_1 = initial length at temperature T_1 °C l_2 = final length at temperature T_2 °C.
Table 7.1 lists average values of coefficient of linear expansion for some materials in the
temperature range 0°C to 100 °C.
Example 7.8: The length of a metal rod at 27 °C is 4 cm. The length increases to 4.02 cm when the metal rod is heated upto 387 °C. Determine the coefficient of linear expansion of the metal rod.
Solution: Given
T_1 = 27 \, ^{\circ}\text{C}

T_2 = 387 \, ^{\circ}\text{C}

l_1 = 4 \, \text{cm} = 4 \times 10^{-2} \, \text{m}

l_2 = 4.02 \, \text{cm} = 4.02 \times 10^{-2} \, \text{m}
We have
\alpha = \frac{l_2 - l_1}{l_1 (T_2 - T_1)}
= \frac{(4.02 - 4.0) \times 10^{-2}}{4 \times 10^{-2} (387 - 27)}
= \frac{0.02 \times 10^{-2}}{4 \times 10^{-2} \times 360}
= 1.39 \times 10^{-5} / ^{\circ}C
Example 7.9: Length of an iron rod at temperature 27 \,^{\circ}\text{C} is 4.256 \,\text{m}. Find the temperature at which the length of the same rod increases to 4.268 \,\text{m}.(\alpha for iron = 1.2 \times 10^{-5} \,\text{K}^{-1})
Solution: Given
T_1 = 27 \,^{\circ}\text{C}, \ l_1 = 4.256 \,\text{m},

l_2 = 4.268 \,\text{m}, \ \alpha = 1.2 \times 10^{-5} \,\text{K}^{-1}
We have
\alpha = \frac{l_2 - l_1}{l_1(T_2 - T_1)} = \frac{l_2 - l_1}{l_1T_2 - l_1T_1}
\therefore l_1T_2 = l_1T_1 + \frac{l_2 - l_1}{\alpha}
T_2 = \frac{1}{l_1} \left[ l_1T_1 + \frac{l_2 - l_1}{\alpha} \right]
= \frac{1}{4.256} \left[ (4.256 \times 27) + \frac{4.268 - 4.256}{1.2 \times 10^{-5}} \right]
= \frac{1}{4.256} \left[ 114.912 + \frac{0.012}{1.2 \times 10^{-5}} \right]
= \frac{1}{4.256} \left[ 114.912 + 1000 \right]
= 261.96 \, ^{\circ}\text{C}

Heat capacity or thermal capacity of a body is the quantity of heat needed to raise or lower the temperature of the whole body by 1°C (or 1K).
... Thermal heat capacity can be written as Heat received or given out
= mass \times 1 \times specific heat capacity
Heat capacity = Q = m \times s --- (7.29)
Heat capacity (thermal capacity) is measured in J/°C.
Example 7.14: Find thermal capacity for a copper block of mass 0.2 kg, if specific heat capacity of copper is 290 J/kg °C.
Solution: Given
m = 0.2 \text{ kg}
s = 290 \text{ J/kg }^{\circ}\text{C}
Thermal capacity = m \times s = 0.2 \text{ kg} \times 290 \text{ J/kg} ^{\circ}\text{C} =58 J/^{\circ}\text{C}

Whenever there is a change in the state of a substance, heat is either absorbed or given out but there is no change in the temperature of the substance.
Latent heat of a substance is the quantity of heat required to change the state of unit mass of the substance without changing its temperature.
Thus if mass m of a substance undergoes a change from one state to the other then the quantity of heat absorbed or released is given by O = mL --- (7.33)
where L is known as latent heat and is characteristic of the substance. Its SI unit is J kg&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;. The value of L depends on the pressure and is usually quoted at one standard atmospheric pressure.
The quantity of heat required to convert unit mass of a substance from its solid state to the liquid state, at its melting point, without any change in its temperature is called its latent heat of fusion (L_{\rm f}).
The quantity of heat required to convert unit mass of a substance from its liquid state to vapour state, at its boiling point without any change in its temperature is called its latent heat of vapourization (L_{\rm u}).
A plot of temperature versus heat energy for a given quantity of water is shown is Fig. 7.11.
From Fig. 7.11, we see that when heat is added (or removed) during a change of state, the temperature remains constant. Also the slopes of the phase lines are not all the same, which indicates that specific heats of the various states are not equal. For water the latent heat
of fusion and vaporisation are L_f = 3.33 \times 10^5 \mathrm{J} \,\mathrm{kg^{-1}} and L_v = 22.6 \times 10^5 \mathrm{J} \,\mathrm{kg^{-1}} respectively. That is 3.33 \times 10^5 \mathrm{J} of heat is needed to melt 1kg of ice at 0 °C and 22.6 \times 10^5 \mathrm{J} of heat is needed to convert 1 kg of water to steam at 100 \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}. Hence, steam at 100 \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} carries 22.6 \times 10^5 \mathrm{J} \,\mathrm{kg^{-1}} more heat than water at 100 \,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}. This is why burns from steam are usually more serious than those from boiling water. Melting points, boiling point and latent heats for various substances are given in Table 7.6.
Example 7.16: When 0.1 kg of ice at 0 °C is mixed with 0.32 kg of water at 35 °C in a container. The resulting temperature of the mixture is 7.8 °C. Calculate the heat of fusion of ice (s_{water} = 4186 \text{ J kg}^{-1} \text{ K}^{-1}).
Solution: Given
m_{\text{ice}} = 0.1 \text{ kg}
m_{\text{water}} = 0.32 \text{ kg}
T_{\text{ice}} = 0 \text{ °C}
T_{\text{water}} = 35 \text{ °C}
T_{\text{F}} = 7.8 \text{ °C}
s_{\text{water}} = 4186 \text{ kg K}^{-1}
Heat lost by water
= m_{\text{water}} s_{\text{water}} (T_{\text{F}} - T_{\text{water}})
= 0.32 kg 4186 J (7.8 - 35) °C
= - 36434.944 J (here negative sign indicates loss of heat energy)
Heat required to melt ice = m_{\text{ice}} L_{\text{f}} = 0.1 \times L_{\text{f}}
Heat required to raise temperature of water (from ice) to final temperature
= m_{ice} s (T - T_{ice})
= 0.1 \text{ kg} \times 4186 \text{ J} \times (7.8 - 0) \text{ C}^{\circ}
= 3265.08 \text{ J}
Head lost = Heat gained
36434.944 = 0.1 L_{f} + 3265.08
L_{f} = \frac{36434.944 - 3265.08}{0.1} = 3316.9864
